WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.120
There’s almost a countless number of easter
eggs scattered across the movies of the MCU.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.180
Some are subtle, while others are far more
obvious and although it's almost impossible

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.620
to list them all, these are a few that you
might just have missed.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.619
For years on end, the worlds of the Avengers
and the X-Men have been kept separate on the

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:21.270
big screen thanks to a conflict in usage rights
between various studios.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.660
While the Avengers assembled at Marvel Studios,
the mutants had their own adventures over

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.580
at Fox — but that doesn't mean there haven’t
been any knowing winks exchanged between the

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.080
two.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.200
During one scene in Iron Man, Tony Stark conducts
a test flight of his new armor for the first

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.820
time and decides to see just how high he can
fly.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:43.140
He asks his A.I. assistant JARVIS what the
altitude record is for a very specific plane:

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.520
“Alright let’s see what this thing can
do.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:46.880
What’s SR-71’s record?

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.240
The record for x-wing flight is 85,000 feet,
sir.”

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.020
That's a real plane, of course, but it also
has a place in the Marvel Universe.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:59.250
The SR-71 Blackbird was used as the model
for the jet flown to missions by the X-Men,

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:04.300
and has often been referred to as "the Blackbird"
whenever it's not being called the X-Jet.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:09.020
Now, Iron Man can't exactly specify that Stark
was referring to Charles Xavier's private

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.820
super plane, but it was a nice nod in the
direction of X-Men fans nonetheless.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:18.900
Captain America: The First Avenger takes Marvel
fans back to World War II not only the era

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.140
in which Steve Rogers became Captain America,
but also the time during which the character

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:24.600
was created in real life.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.520
But he wasn’t the only superhero to come
out of this period.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:32.560
During the Stark Expo scene, eagle-eyed viewers
will be able to spot another classic Marvel

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.760
character who was created back when the company
was still known as Timely Comics.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:42.020
As Steve, Bucky and their dates walk through
the expo, they pass a glass display case with

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:46.320
a costume in it labeled "Dr. Phineas Horton
Presents The Synthetic Man."

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.040
Horton is the scientist who created the original
Human Torch all the way back in Marvel Comics

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:51.540
#1.

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:55.580
This character, who was initially kept in
a glass cylinder much like the one in Captain

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.460
America, remains an important piece of the
foundations of the Marvel Universe.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.080
This particular shot establishes that some
version of him existed in the MCU at some

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.879
point so maybe one day we'll flash back again
and get the full story of Synthetic Man and

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:09.880
his adventures.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:14.360
The Marvel Cinematic Universe may be a world
unto itself, but it exists in the greater

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:19.120
realm of Marvel Entertainment, which itself
contains a vast multiverse of various worlds

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.600
featuring many alternate versions of its characters.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:28.160
The MCU even has its own designated reality
number: 199999.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.840
This places it within the greater Marvel multiverse,
meaning that whether we'll ever see it or

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.989
not there’s a cosmic link between the cinematic
versions of these characters and their prime

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:39.100
continuity comic book counterparts.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:44.579
That link was further emphasized in Thor:
The Dark World, when Erik Selvig laid out

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.520
his understanding of reality on a giant chalkboard
during his time in a mental institution.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:54.660
Among his scribblings are the words: "616
Universe” — the official designation of

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.600
the main Marvel Comics universe.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.040
“Any questions?”

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:02.340
“Yeah.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.200
Can I have my shoe back?”

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:09.260
Although Stan Lee’s cameos in Marvel movies
have become practically legendary, Lee isn’t

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.980
the only Marvel luminary to pop up in the
MCU at one point or another.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.640
One of the most notable instances of this
came during Captain America: The Winter Soldier.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.360
As Alexander Pierce is questioning the Winter
Soldier, a team of handlers are standing around

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:22.860
the room.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.840
One of them, seen on the left, is Ed Brubaker
an Eisner Award-winning comic book writer

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:32.440
responsible for resurrecting Bucky Barnes
as The Winter Soldier in Captain America #1,

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:33.470
back in 2005.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:38.560
Before Brubaker joined Captain America, Bucky
had been dead for decades, and was long considered

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.040
one of the rare comics characters who would
never return from the grave.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.620
Without Brubaker, the MCU would be a very
different place indeed so it's nice to see

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.600
him get a little moment of recognition here.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.699
No easter egg within the entirety of the MCU
is quite as bountiful as the Collector’s

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.380
hideaway on Knowhere in Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:01.920
His massive treasure trove of artifacts and
living specimens seems to go on forever — and

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.579
if you're willing to be a little liberal with
the pause button, you're likely to notice

00:04:05.579 --> 00:04:07.609
quite a few interesting things.

00:04:07.609 --> 00:04:11.340
“Very well, then let us see what you’ve
brought.”

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.799
Among his items are a Chitauri warrior from
The Avengers, a Dark Elf from Thor: The Dark

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.900
World, Cosmo the spacedog, and, of course,
Howard the Duck.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.139
However, there's one more find tucked away
in that room that could have wide-reaching

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.440
implications for the future of the MCU.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.200
It’s possible to spot a certain cocoon-like
object that writer/director James Gunn later

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:37.120
hinted was the gestation place of Adam Warlock,
a character further teased in a post-credits

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.360
scene from Vol. 2.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.900
As it turns out, the two objects might not
be the exact same cocoon, but the visual cues

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:44.300
are difficult to ignore.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.400
Adam Warlock has massive cosmic implications
for Marvel's future, so if he does show up

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.200
at some point, we can probably tie it all
back to this.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:57.380
Some easter eggs are so obscure that you'll
only recognize them if you carefully read

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.120
one very specific comic book.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.580
That's the case here with this gem from Captain
America: Civil War.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:07.250
At Peggy Carter’s funeral, her niece Sharon
delivers the eulogy and passes on some words

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:09.080
of wisdom her aunt once shared with her.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.600
“Even if the whole world is telling you
to move.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:17.940
It is your duty to plant yourself like a tree
look them in the eye and say no.”

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.220
It's a powerful moment.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:23.220
For certain comic book readers, however, it
was made all the more powerful by the fact

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:29.349
that it's a speech lifted largely from Amazing
Spider-Man #537, a comic tying in to the original

00:05:29.349 --> 00:05:34.020
Civil War event in which a very similar speech
is given by none other than Captain America

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:34.540
himself.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:38.380
Screenwriters Christopher Markus and Stephen
McFeely definitely did their homework with

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:38.920
this one.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:44.699
Some easter eggs may begin as an amusing idea,
but sadly just don't come together and fail

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.460
to ever reach a satisfying pay-off.

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:51.300
In the MCU, where some parts of the universe
are attempting to work with the same concepts

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.200
as others, this can happen a lot.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:58.080
For example, it was the case with one particular
hidden gem in Doctor Strange, which has been

00:05:58.099 --> 00:06:01.449
semi-retconned by the events of Hulu's Runaways
TV series.

00:06:01.449 --> 00:06:05.740
At various points in the film, we see glimpses
of the Hong Kong Sanctum, where sorcerers

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:10.090
work to guard that particular corner of the
world from a host of magical threats.

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:15.190
Among them is a staff-wielding character named
Tina Minoru, played in Doctor Strange by Linda

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:16.180
Louise Duan.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.939
We only know her name because she's listed
in the credits, but Minoru happens to be the

00:06:19.939 --> 00:06:25.419
mother of Nico Minoru, one of the main stars
of Runaways, who eventually wields her mother's

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:26.240
staff herself.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:30.900
Of course, Tina Minoru was recast for Runaways
but it's still a nice touch.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:35.460
Some Marvel characters might not be ready
for prime time yet, but are still loved by

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:39.280
enough fans and filmmakers to make at least
a brief appearance in the MCU.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:43.960
One instance of this happening occurs during
Thor: Ragnarok, when we see the Grandmaster's

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:46.220
palace on Sakaar for the first time.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.439
This towering structure is adorned with various
shimmering statues depicting the heads of

00:06:50.439 --> 00:06:54.389
the champions that have fought in the arena
and while the Hulk head under construction

00:06:54.389 --> 00:06:58.780
might end up dominating the whole thing, the
heads that have already been built are particularly

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:00.560
fascinating for comic book fans.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:07.060
They include Bi-Beast, Man-Thing, Ares and
even Beta Ray Bill, a longtime ally of Thor

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:09.980
who fans have been hoping to see on the big
screen for years.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.940
We may not see any of these characters actually
living and breathing in the MCU anytime soon,

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:19.340
but these statues were enough to let us know
they exist and that's a good start.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:25.100
Avengers: Infinity War is very loosely based
on the 1991 Marvel Comics event series The

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:26.160
Infinity Gauntlet.

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.630
While the plot of the movie differs significantly
from its origin story, the film bears many

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:32.129
nods and winks to the text.

00:07:32.129 --> 00:07:36.150
For example, Bruce Banner crashes through
the roof of Doctor Strange's Sanctum, echoing

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:39.060
the moment when the Silver Surfer did the
same thing in the comics.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:41.860
“I understood that reference.”

00:07:43.169 --> 00:07:47.320
Of all the moments that made Gauntlet fans
happy, however, one that really stands out

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:52.189
comes in the film's very last shot, when Thanos
sits down at his humble farm to "watch the

00:07:52.189 --> 00:07:54.380
sun rise on a grateful universe."

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:58.939
As he walks through the hut, you'll see something
standing amid the crops growing outside: a

00:07:58.939 --> 00:08:01.530
scarecrow made from his old battle armor.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:06.199
At the end of Infinity Gauntlet, Thanos gives
up his quest for ultimate power after the

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:09.500
battle, takes up farming and makes a scarecrow
with his armor.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:14.780
These may be two very different ways of arriving
at the same image, but it's a great reference

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:15.409
nonetheless.

